                0.382
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 10.3: (a) Convex mirror; (b) Its representation"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.397,
            0.882,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "A spherical mirror which has a reflecting surface that curves outwards is called a **convex mirror** (Fig. 10.3a). Its schematic representation is shown in Fig. 10.3b."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.455,
            0.872,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "In the representation of both the mirrors, the non-reflecting surface of the mirror is shown as shaded."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.506,
            0.436,
            0.528
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A step further"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.265,
            0.544,
            0.859,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": "The shape of a spherical mirror is such that it can be thought of as a part of an imaginary hollow sphere. However, remember that spherical mirrors are not made by slicing a hollow glass sphere. Instead, they are created by grinding and polishing a flat glass piece into a curved surface. If a reflective coating (like a thin layer of aluminium) is applied on the outer curved surface, it forms a concave mirror. If the coating is applied on the inner curved surface, it forms a convex mirror."
        },
        {
